MARS on Linux 7.2 / 7.3 in QEMU microvm
======================================

Run from the repository root, without root privileges:

    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc
    python3 test_suite/qemu/replicate.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc
    python3 test_suite/qemu/replicate.py 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc 7.2.9-2-cachyos

Substitute the exact installed kernel releases to test another build. Each
kernel needs /lib/modules/<release>/build, vmlinuz, and its module tree.
The build directory must contain the kernel configuration and Module.symvers.
Match the kernel's toolchain: the runner selects LLVM=1 for Clang kernels.

Host prerequisites: x86-64 Linux, Python 3, QEMU system x86-64 with microvm,
KVM access, make, the matching C compiler/binutils or LLVM, Perl and its core
modules, cpio, gzip, kmod, e2fsprogs, iproute2, util-linux, GNU coreutils,
bash, grep, sed, awk, findutils and pstree. No downloaded guest distribution,
host module installation, TAP device, sudo or host block device is used.
The target kernel needs modules, initramfs/gzip, devtmpfs, procfs, sysfs,
ext4, virtio-mmio/block/net, IPv4/TCP, sysctl and the MARS crypto dependencies.

The single-node runner also supports --accel tcg when KVM is unavailable
(increase --timeout for software emulation). Replication uses KVM. Each guest
has 2 vCPUs and 2 GiB RAM. Each run creates a sparse 32 GiB metadata image
and a 128 MiB backing disk. Two-node tests create one pair per guest.
--workdir accepts a new directory; otherwise a temporary directory is used.
Artifacts are retained at the printed path, including build.log, console.log,
command.json, mars.ko under source/kernel/, initramfs, and disk images.
Remove that directory after inspection to reclaim the space.

QEMU directly boots the selected vmlinuz and generated initramfs with the
microvm machine. ACPI is disabled, the RTC supplies cluster timestamps, and
isa-debug-exit terminates the test without depending on firmware shutdown.
The single-node test uses QEMU user networking. The replication test joins
two virtual NICs over a private localhost socket and bootstraps only their
cluster UUID/IP records; resource metadata and data travel through MARS TCP.

Single-node coverage:
  - Build/link, module load, sysctl registration and Lamport clock access.
  - Cluster/resource creation and exported disk capacity.
  - Random writes with fsync, direct reads, byte comparison, demotion,
    detach, unload, and comparison against the backing device.
  - Reload, promotion, ext4 format/mount, fsynced file write, unmount,
    detach/unload, and read-only verification on the backing filesystem.

Two-node coverage:
  - Metadata exchange, resource join and full 128 MiB initial synchronization.
  - A new 1 MiB pattern written after initial sync, transaction-log shipping
    and replay, then direct comparison on the secondary backing device.

The runners fail on timeouts, guest failures, kernel warnings/oops/panics or
missing success markers. The pair is terminated after replication succeeds;
graceful demotion and unload are covered by the single-node runner.
--prepare-only on run.py builds the artifacts without starting QEMU.

Validation uses the default MARS configuration on the named CachyOS kernels,
not an upstream vanilla build. These are functional compatibility tests;
they do not measure sustained throughput, simulate power loss, or cover
every debug configuration and legacy kernel.

Verified on 2026-10-14 with QEMU 11.1.2/KVM and Clang 23.1.1:

    7.2.9-2-cachyos                 single-node + ext4: PASS
    7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc          single-node + ext4: PASS
    7.2.9 -> 7.3.0-rc6             initial sync + log replay: PASS
    7.3.0-rc6 -> 7.2.9             initial sync + log replay: PASS
